7592186064991 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064991 #edb/ref 1061 12774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064991 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95f45e70-98bb-552e-8394-58e2d30a7385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064991 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065167 #edb/ref 1061 24870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065167 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4737b469-c8a4-5051-b73e-2b3bd7b686fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065167 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065346 #edb/ref 1064 "The **mean value theorem** states that for any function that is continuous and differentiable on some interval, there is some point where the derivative of the function is equal to the slope between the endpoints. More precisely, that is:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.\n> $$\n\nLet's illustrate this idea with an example. Consider the graph of the function \n\n$$\ny = f(x) = 6 - \\dfrac 4 x\n$$\n\nthat's defined on the closed interval $[1,4].$\n\n![](images/fd/fd4d75f2ed0db427c2e3bd41335ceb302c3126781f595c8c21ec914e50f82f64.png)\n\nThe line $l_1$ is the secant line that intersects the curve at the endpoints of the interval. The mean value theorem guarantees that there is some special point inside the interval where the tangent line has the same slope as $l_1.$ \n\nIn the diagram above, the special point is $(2,4),$ and the tangent line $l_2$ at this point is parallel to the secant line $l_1.$\n\nTo double-check that $l_1$ and $l_2$ are parallel, we can compute their slopes. The line $l_1$ passes through the points $(1,f(1))$ and $(4,f(4)),$ so its slope is given by \n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} =  \\dfrac{5-2}{4-1} = 1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the line $l_2$ can be found by calculating $f'(2).$\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{4}{x^2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(2) = 1\n$$\n\nSince the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ have the same slope, they are parallel." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065346 #edb/ref 1065 2659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065346 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "baf78b35-04c6-5d1c-913f-e0a3da70cd8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065346 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428794 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428794 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 288->3563. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428794 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050359 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428795 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428795 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the c